def methods(d):
    s=d['summary'];cell=s['cell_status'];nuc=s['nucleus_status']
    rows=''.join(f'<tr><td>{status}</td><td>{cell.get(status,0):,}</td><td>{nuc.get(status,0):,}</td></tr>' for status in sorted(set(cell)|set(nuc)))
    hashes='<br>'.join(f'{c}: <code>{v["sha256"]}</code>' for c,v in d['source']['sources'].items())
    return f'''<h2>Source and alignment</h2><p>Phase and RFP ZIP files were downloaded from the FLD_102 20× links on the <a href="https://davislab.github.io/LFCT-dataset/mcf10a.html">dataset website</a>. Each contains 385 uint16 TIFFs of 1224 × 904 pixels. Numeric filename suffixes 1–385 map to original XML frames 0–384 by subtracting one. Both channels have every index; no image frames were dropped. macOS resource forks were excluded during extraction (385 per archive). ZIP order was never used for time ordering. The first image pair is severely defocused and the source XML has no detections there; it remains in the viewer.</p><p>Coordinates are the original pixel POSITION_X / POSITION_Y, with no translation, rotation, rescaling, or registration correction. Alignment was visually checked against the raw images and quantified by centroid containment. These whole-cell tracking positions need not be nuclear centroids. Timing is reported in frames because the XML 1-second interval is inconsistent with the dataset description (10–15 minutes).</p>
<h2>Segmentation</h2><p>Cellpose-SAM 4.0.6, PyTorch {d['model']['phase']['torch']}, cached pretrained <code>cpsam</code> weights in the existing <code>cpose</code> Conda environment. Inference ran on NVIDIA A30 GPUs through SLURM with QoS small, four CPU cores and 16 GB RAM per job. The four known CUDA-failing nodes were excluded. Model SHA-256: <code>{d['model']['phase']['model_sha256']}</code>.</p><p>Each phase image was segmented independently as a grayscale cell image using the original uint16 data. Each RFP image was segmented independently as a grayscale nuclear image. Images were processed in 2D at native resolution, without diameter-driven downsampling, inversion, augmentation, temporal smoothing, or retracking. Flow threshold 0.4; cell-probability threshold 0; minimum mask area 15 pixels; maximum mask area fraction 0.4; batch size 8; tiles 256 pixels with overlap 0.1; resampling enabled. Phase normalization used Cellpose’s per-image 1st/99th percentiles. RFP used 1st/99th percentile local normalization with a 128-pixel block size. Seed 0.</p><p>Small pilot comparisons examined default RFP normalization, local normalization, and local normalization after Gaussian background subtraction. Local normalization yielded 18 masks versus 16 at XML frame 192 and 84 versus 82 at frame 384. Background subtraction reduced late-frame detections to 55 and was rejected. These are qualitative pilot choices, not independently validated optimal parameters. No per-frame parameter tuning was performed.</p>
<h2>Assignment rules</h2><ol><li>Preserve every source detection, Spot ID, Track ID, track name, and track edge. Match per frame and use each mask at most once. Containment samples the pixel nearest the unmodified subpixel tracking position.</li><li>A mask containing exactly one tracked centroid is assigned to it (<code>contained</code>). A mask containing multiple centroids is reserved and left unassigned (<code>shared_mask</code>); it is never copied or artificially divided.</li><li>For a centroid outside all masks, allow the nearest mask only when its boundary lies within 15 pixels, it is unoccupied, and it is at least 3 pixels closer than the next candidate. Conflicting proposals remain unmatched (<code>nearby</code>, <code>ambiguous_nearby</code>, <code>competing_centroids</code>, or <code>nearest_mask_occupied</code>). Distances use unsimplified mask boundary pixels.</li><li>Nuclei follow the same rules. A remaining centroid with a uniquely assigned cell may additionally receive a nucleus only when exactly one nuclear mask has at least 50% of its area inside that cell and is unassigned and not shared by multiple centroids (<code>cell_overlap</code>). No nuclear boundary is synthesized where RFP segmentation provides none.</li><li>Mask edges touching the field boundary are retained and flagged in the inventory. Unassigned masks are retained on disk and available as dashed overlays; they are excluded from the default assigned outlines. No source track or centroid is excluded.</li></ol>
<h2>Results and issues</h2><p>{s['matched_cells']:,} / {s['tracked_detections']:,} detections have assigned cell outlines; {s['matched_nuclei']:,} have nuclear outlines. The original XML lacked polygon coordinates for {s['missing_original_outlines']:,} of these detections. There are {s['unassigned_cell_masks']:,} unassigned cell-mask instances and {s['unassigned_nuclear_masks']:,} unassigned nuclear-mask instances, summed over time. {s['low_nucleus_cell_overlap']:,} assigned cell/nucleus pairs have less than 50% nuclear overlap and are flagged for review.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Assignment status</th><th>Cell detections</th><th>Nuclear detections</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows}</tbody></table><p>Weak or absent-looking nuclear signal, uneven illumination, and saturated bright nuclei occur in the RFP images. The QC saturation fraction is a proxy counting pixels ≥65504 (the observed upper plateau), not a calibrated detector saturation measurement. Missing nuclear segmentation is not proof that a cell lacks a nucleus. Crowded late frames contain segmentation splits/merges and unsupported centroids; ambiguous matches are left unresolved. Nearest-boundary and cell-overlap assignments are heuristic and should be reviewed. No manual corrections or automated lineage repairs were applied.</p><p>TrackMate IDs describe connected tracking components and can include many simultaneous cells after branching. Fill colors default to these exact IDs. Optional trajectory-branch coloring uses the first Spot ID of each uninterrupted one-to-one chain as a derived color key, breaking at splits or merges. Original IDs/names remain displayed. Eight colors are reused, with nearest-neighbor graph coloring favoring different colors locally; identical original Track IDs necessarily share a color.</p>
<h2>Display and reproducibility</h2><p>Raw viewing backgrounds use independent per-frame 1st/99.5th percentile contrast and JPEG quality 55. They are for visual review, not quantitative intensity measurements or temporal brightness comparisons. Segmentation uses the original uint16 TIFFs. Viewer polygons simplify pixel boundaries by at most 0.65 pixels; exact masks are retained as lossless uint16 TIFFs. Counts and distances are calculated from exact masks, not display polygons.</p><p>The original XML is unchanged. <code>assignments.csv</code> maps each original detection to cell/nuclear mask labels and records statuses, distances, and issues. <code>mask_inventory.csv</code> records all masks, including unmatched and edge-touching objects. <code>frame_qc.csv</code> gives frame-level counts; <code>viewer_data.json</code> includes the reassigned and original contours. Download these tables from the panel below. Generation scripts and SLURM workflow are retained under <code>scripts/</code> and <code>workflow/</code>.</p><p>Input archive SHA-256 hashes:<br>{hashes}<br>Original XML SHA-256: <code>{d['source']['xml']['sha256']}</code>.</p>'''


def render(payload):
    payload['tables']={name:(OUT/name).read_text() for name in ['assignments.csv','frame_qc.csv','mask_inventory.csv']}
    content=methods(payload)
    payload['backgrounds']={c:[f'lfct_mcf10a_fld102_cpsam_assets/backgrounds/{c}_{f["frame"]:04d}.jpg' for f in payload['frames']] for c in ['phase','rfp']}
    html=TEMPLATE.replace('__METHODS__',content).replace('__DATA__',json.dumps(payload,separators=(',',':')).replace('</','<\\/'))
    (ROOT/'reports/lfct_mcf10a_fld102_cpsam_local.html').write_text(html.replace('<button id="save">Save PNG</button>', '<button id="save" disabled title="Use the standalone report to export PNGs">Save PNG</button>'))
    for c in ['phase','rfp']:
        payload['backgrounds'][c]=['data:image/jpeg;base64,'+base64.b64encode((OUT/'backgrounds'/f'{c}_{f["frame"]:04d}.jpg').read_bytes()).decode() for f in payload['frames']]
    html=TEMPLATE.replace('__METHODS__',content).replace('__DATA__',json.dumps(payload,separators=(',',':')).replace('</','<\\/'))
    path=ROOT/'reports/lfct_mcf10a_fld102_cpsam.html';path.write_text(html)
    (OUT/'methods.html').write_text(content)
    print(f'Wrote self-contained viewer: {path} ({path.stat().st_size/1024**2:.1f} MiB)',flush=True)

if __name__=='__main__':render(json.loads((OUT/'viewer_data.json').read_text()))
